# Fail Fast

Bugs must surface at the source, loudly, during development — not as an NRE three frames later in unrelated code.

Reference-type parameters are non-null by contract — see the **non-null-parameter-contract** rule. Validate at boundaries; callees use parameters directly.

## Callers: no silent bail on missing values

If code **expects** a value to exist (dependency, payload, serialized ref) and it is null or missing, do **not** bare `return`. Log with context first.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — bug is invisible
if (data == null) return;

// ✅ GOOD — surfaces misconfiguration or bad input
if (data == null)
{
    Debug.LogError($"[CombatSystem] ApplyDamage — payload deserialized to null (id={eventId})");
    return;
}
```

Use a consistent prefix: `[ClassName] MethodName — reason`, with useful context (ids, state).

| Level | When |
|-------|------|
| **Error** | Required for this code path; skipping means wrong state, lost event, or broken feature |
| **Warning** | Unexpected but recoverable, or possibly optional |

Silent return is OK only for: intentional control flow (debounce, dedup, idempotent guards), lifecycle (`this == null` after async, teardown), legitimately optional fields, and explicit product choices.

## Producers: never return null to signal failure

A method expected to return a value must not return `null` for "not found". Throw with a descriptive message at the source.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — leaks a null that NREs elsewhere
public Unit GetUnit(int id)
{
    if (!_units.TryGetValue(id, out var unit)) return null;
    return unit;
}

// ✅ GOOD — throws at the source with context
public Unit GetUnit(int id)
{
    if (!_units.TryGetValue(id, out var unit))
    {
        throw new InvalidOperationException(
            $"[UnitRegistry] GetUnit — no unit with id {id}. Known: [{string.Join(", ", _units.Keys)}].");
    }
    return unit;
}
```

When absence is a **normal** outcome, expose a `Try*` API instead of a nullable getter:

```csharp
public bool TryGetUnit(int id, out Unit unit) { ... }
```

Pair the two: throwing `Get*` for must-exist paths, `Try*` for may-be-absent paths. No "log error then `return null`" half-measures — if it's an error, throw.

Returning null is allowed only when null is a genuine contract value: parse/deserialize results, "no match is normal" lookups (prefer `Try*` even there), documented "none" sentinels.

## No defensive null checks on internal code

Trust internal invariants. Validate only at system boundaries (constructors, Awake for serialized refs, user input, network payloads). **Do not** re-validate the same ref in every method it is passed to.

For fields guaranteed by init order, use directly. For "should never happen" states in development, `Debug.Assert` is OK — but prefer fixing the API contract over sprinkling asserts.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — hides the bug
if (target == null) return;

// ✅ GOOD — fails loudly in development (field invariant, not a parameter)
Debug.Assert(_target != null, "[Turret] Fire — target must be assigned before Fire()");
_target.ApplyDamage(amount);
```

## Agent checklist

- [ ] **No `if (param == null) return` on reference-type parameters** (see non-null-parameter-contract rule)
- [ ] Expected-to-exist null → `Debug.LogError`/`LogWarning` with `[ClassName] Method — reason` context, never silent `return`
- [ ] Must-exist lookups throw; may-be-absent lookups expose `Try*` — no `return null` for failure
- [ ] No new defensive null checks on internal code paths; validate once at boundary
- [ ] No silent `catch { }` — log at minimum
